# Young adults (part a): facilitator scripts, tasks and witness stories.
R = {
'YA-1-1': dict(
  saint="C. S. Lewis: an Oxford atheist who called himself the most reluctant convert in England. (Not a Catholic, but a witness many Catholics read.)",
  tryit=["Write down the strongest reason you have doubted God.", "Write the strongest reason you have hoped.", "Trade with a partner and respond honestly, without fixing."],
  saintStory="Clive Staples Lewis was born in Belfast in 1898. His mother died of cancer when he was nine, after he had prayed for her recovery, and he began to drift from faith. By his mid-teens he was an atheist. He fought in the First World War, was wounded, and then became a brilliant student and a fellow at Oxford, and a rationalist who thought religion was a beautiful but false myth.\nBut he kept being ambushed by what he called Joy: a piercing longing, set off by a line of poetry or a northern landscape, that nothing in the world satisfied. And his best friends at Oxford, including J. R. R. Tolkien, were Christians. In 1929, he later wrote, “I gave in, and admitted that God was God, and knelt and prayed: perhaps, that night, the most dejected and reluctant convert in all England.” That was belief in God. Belief in Christ came in September 1931, after a late-night conversation with Tolkien and Hugo Dyson on a walk at Magdalen College, in which Tolkien argued that the Christian story was a myth that had really happened. A few days later, riding to the zoo in a motorcycle sidecar, Lewis realized that he believed.\nHe became an Anglican, and the author of Mere Christianity, The Screwtape Letters and the Chronicles of Narnia. He died on November 22, 1963.",
  script=dict(
    open="Welcome the group, and ask each person to answer in one sentence: “What is a question about God that you have not been able to put down?” Write them on a board or sheet without comment. Say, “Tonight we are not here to settle these. We are here to see whether the question is a door.”",
    teach=["Say: “The Catechism begins its whole treatment of faith with a surprising claim: ‘The desire for God is written in the human heart, because man is created by God and for God.’ (CCC 27) It continues: ‘Man is by nature and vocation a religious being. Coming from God, going toward God, man lives a fully human life only if he freely lives by his bond with God.’ Almost every culture in history has had prayer, sacrifice and some account of the divine. The Church doesn’t treat this as proof, but as a pointer. Hunger points to food; thirst to water; the desire for God, perhaps, to God. Lewis argued this in Mere Christianity: ‘If I find in myself a desire which no experience in this world can satisfy, the most probable explanation is that I was made for another world.’”", "Say: “The Church also teaches that reason can know God. CCC 31-35 names the ways: the world (its order, beauty, contingency), and the human person (conscience, longing for truth and happiness, openness to the infinite). First Vatican Council: God ‘can be known with certainty from the created world by the natural light of human reason.’ Aquinas’s five ways and others are arguments, not coercion. They show faith is reasonable. They aren’t the same as faith. Faith is a gift and a free response to a God who reveals himself. And the Church admits the obstacles (CCC 29): ‘the rebellion against evil in the world, religious ignorance or indifference, the cares and riches of this world, the scandal of bad example on the part of believers.’ Many people leave not because the arguments fail, but because of wounds.”", "Say: “Doubt and seeking. The Church does not treat honest questions as sin. The father in Mark 9:24 prays, ‘I believe; help my unbelief!’ Augustine’s Confessions is a prayer from a restless heart. A seeker’s task is to be honest: follow the evidence where it leads, keep asking, notice what you are afraid of, and be willing to pray, even tentatively: ‘God, if you are there…’ Jesus promised: ‘Seek and you will find.’ (Matthew 7:7) Lewis’s conversion shows how God works through friends, books, longing and hard thinking all together. If you believe already, the task is to understand why, and to give an account to others (1 Peter 3:15). If you don’t, the invitation is to keep the question open.”"],
    ask=["What does the Catechism claim about the desire for God? (It is written in the human heart because we are made by God and for God.)", "Name two kinds of evidence the Church points to for God. (The created world and the human person: conscience, longing for truth and happiness.)", "Is honest doubt a sin? (No; the Church welcomes honest seeking.)"],
    practice=["In pairs, each person names a moment they felt a longing that nothing in the world satisfied. Listen without fixing.", "Individually, write down your strongest reason to doubt and your strongest reason to hope. Swap with a partner, who writes a one-sentence honest response to each.", "Together, read Psalm 63:1-8 slowly.", "Each person writes a two-line prayer, however tentative: “God, if you are there…” or “God, you are here, and…”"],
    close="Close with silence for a minute, then the Our Father together. Sign of the Cross. Invite everyone to read the first chapter of Book 1 of Augustine’s Confessions or the first part of Mere Christianity before next time.")),

'YA-1-2': dict(
  saint="Andrei Rublev: a Russian monk who painted three angels at a table as a window on the Trinity.",
  tryit=["Look at the Rublev icon for five silent minutes.", "Write what you notice.", "Say which truth about the Trinity it shows."],
  saintStory="Andrei Rublev was a monk and icon painter, born around 1360 in Russia, in a time of war and the Mongol domination. He lived at monasteries in Moscow, and worked with the famous painter Theophanes the Greek. He was gentle and quiet, a man of prayer.\nAround 1411, he was asked to paint an icon in honor of St. Sergius of Radonezh, who had founded the Trinity Monastery. He chose the scene from Genesis 18, where Abraham welcomes three mysterious visitors at Mamre, whom the Church Fathers saw as a sign of the Trinity. Rublev painted three angels around a table, their heads inclined toward one another, a cup at the center. The circle formed by their bodies draws the viewer in, and there is an open place at the table.\nThe icon became the most famous in Russia. Rublev died around 1428. The Russian Orthodox Church canonized him in 1988. The icon shows the Trinity not as a puzzle but as a communion of love, with room for us.",
  script=dict(
    open="Place an image of Rublev’s Trinity where all can see (printed or on a screen). Say nothing for two minutes. Then ask, “What do you notice first? What draws your eye?” Take answers without correcting.",
    teach=["Say: “The Trinity is the central mystery of the Christian faith: one God in three Persons, the Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit. The Catechism: ‘The Trinity is One. We do not confess three Gods, but one God in three persons, the ‘consubstantial Trinity.’ The divine persons do not share the one divinity among themselves but each of them is God whole and entire.’ (CCC 253) Three statements must be held together: God is one; each Person is fully God; the Persons are really distinct, as the Father begets, the Son is begotten, and the Spirit proceeds. A mystery is not a contradiction: it is a truth too rich for us to grasp fully. We can know it only because God revealed it.”", "Say: “Where does it come from? The Old Testament speaks of God’s oneness (Deuteronomy 6:4) and hints at plurality (Genesis 1:26; the three visitors of Genesis 18). In the New Testament, the Trinity is revealed in Jesus. At his baptism, the Father speaks, the Son is baptized, the Spirit descends like a dove (Matthew 3:16-17). Jesus commands baptism ‘in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit.’ (Matthew 28:19) Paul’s blessing: ‘The grace of the Lord Jesus Christ and the love of God and the fellowship of the Holy Spirit be with you all.’ (2 Corinthians 13:14) John 1:1: ‘the Word was with God, and the Word was God.’ The Church spent centuries finding language, at Nicaea (325) and Constantinople (381). Words like ‘person’ and ‘substance’ were tools to protect the truth.”", "Say: “Why does it matter? Because of what it says about God: God is not a lonely monad but a communion of love. ‘God is love’ (1 John 4:8) means that from all eternity the Father loves the Son in the Spirit. Creation and salvation flow from that love: the Father sends the Son, and they send the Spirit. We are made in the image of this communion; our lives are meant to be relationships of self-gift. Prayer is participation in the Trinity: we pray to the Father, through the Son, in the Holy Spirit. Every Sign of the Cross is a creed. Rublev’s icon: the table has an open place, which invites us into the life of God. St. Patrick’s shamrock is a familiar teaching image, but analogies fall short; the Church avoids the heresies of modalism (one God in three masks) and tritheism (three gods).”"],
    ask=["State the Trinity in one sentence. (One God in three divine Persons: Father, Son and Holy Spirit.)", "Where in the New Testament is the Trinity most visible? (The baptism of Jesus; the baptismal formula in Matthew 28:19.)", "Why does the Trinity matter for how we live? (God is a communion of love, and we are made in his image.)"],
    practice=["Sit in silence before the icon for five minutes. Then write three things you noticed and one question.", "In pairs, explain to each other what you think each Person of the Trinity does (the Father, the Son, the Spirit), and where you find it hard.", "Together, pray the Sign of the Cross slowly, naming each Person.", "Individually, write a two-sentence answer to a friend who asks, “Isn’t the Trinity just three gods?”"],
    close="Pray the Glory Be slowly and then the Sign of the Cross. Invite the group to make the Sign of the Cross thoughtfully once a day this week, naming the Persons.")),

'YA-1-3': dict(
  saint="Martha of Bethany: the busy sister who confessed, before Lazarus rose, that Jesus is the Christ.",
  tryit=["Write your own answer to “Who do you say that I am?”", "Say what it costs you to say it.", "Say where it shows up in your week."],
  saintStory="Martha lived in Bethany, near Jerusalem, with her sister Mary and brother Lazarus. Jesus often stayed in their house. Luke records the famous scene: Martha was “distracted with much serving,” and complained to Jesus that Mary had left her to serve alone. Jesus said, “Martha, Martha, you are anxious and troubled about many things; one thing is needful. Mary has chosen the good portion.” Martha is often remembered for this mild rebuke.\nBut John tells a deeper story. When Lazarus fell ill, the sisters sent word to Jesus, who delayed. By the time he arrived, Lazarus had been dead four days. Martha went out to meet him, and said, “Lord, if you had been here, my brother would not have died.” Jesus said, “Your brother will rise again,” and then, “I am the resurrection and the life; he who believes in me, though he die, yet shall he live.” He asked, “Do you believe this?”\nShe answered, “Yes, Lord; I believe that you are the Christ, the Son of God, he who is coming into the world.” This confession echoes Peter’s. Shortly after, Jesus raised Lazarus. Martha served at the supper afterward. The Church honors her on July 29.",
  script=dict(
    open="Give each person an index card. Ask them to write, anonymously, one sentence beginning, “Jesus is…” Collect, shuffle and read them aloud one by one, without comment. Notice the range together.",
    teach=["Say: “Jesus asked his disciples at Caesarea Philippi, ‘Who do men say that the Son of man is?’ and then, ‘But who do you say that I am?’ (Matthew 16:13-15) The whole Christian faith turns on the second question. The Church answers in the Creed: Jesus is the eternal Son of God, born of the Virgin Mary, truly God and truly man, who died and rose to save us. The Gospels preserve what Jesus said and did that provoked this question: he taught with authority; forgave sins; healed; claimed to be Lord of the Sabbath; said, ‘Before Abraham was, I am’; accepted worship from Thomas, ‘My Lord and my God’; and was killed for claiming to be the Son of God.’”", "Say: “C. S. Lewis posed the argument well: ‘I am trying here to prevent anyone saying the really foolish thing that people often say about Him: ‘I’m ready to accept Jesus as a great moral teacher, but I don’t accept His claim to be God.’ That is the one thing we must not say. A man who was merely a man and said the sort of things Jesus said would not be a great moral teacher. He would either be a lunatic—on a level with the man who says he is a poached egg—or else he would be the Devil of Hell. You must make your choice.’ Critics say Lewis ignores the option that the stories are legend. But the Gospels were written within decades by people who knew eyewitnesses, and the early Church’s worship of Jesus was very early (Philippians 2:6-11). The Resurrection is the decisive evidence.”", "Say: “Jesus is not just an idea; he is a person who asks, ‘Do you believe this?’ The Church’s faith is a personal trust. The Catechism (CCC 426): ‘At the heart of catechesis we find, in essence, a Person, the Person of Jesus of Nazareth.’ How do we come to know him? Reading the Gospels (the best way is to read one slowly), the sacraments, prayer, the community of believers, and acts of service (Matthew 25). Martha confessed her faith before she saw the miracle. Our answer to Jesus’ question may be partial and still growing. The invitation of this course is to take the question seriously and to let him answer it in us. Peter’s response (‘You are the Christ’) is a gift of the Father (Matthew 16:17). Pray for that gift.”"],
    ask=["What is the central question of the Gospels? (“Who do you say that I am?”)", "What did Martha confess? (That Jesus is the Christ, the Son of God.)", "What is Lewis’s argument in short? (Jesus can’t be merely a good teacher; he was either Lord, a liar or a lunatic.)"],
    practice=["Read John 11:17-27 aloud, slowly, in turns.", "Write your own answer to the question, “Who do you say that I am?” in three sentences, as honest as you can.", "In pairs, share one sentence from your answer, and what it costs you to say it.", "Choose one Gospel passage to read this week, and write down which question of Jesus you will bring to it."],
    close="Close by praying together Martha’s words slowly: “Yes, Lord; I believe that you are the Christ, the Son of God.” Sign of the Cross. Encourage the group to read Mark’s Gospel, a chapter a day.")),

'YA-2-1': dict(
  saint="Paula of Rome: a widow who learned Hebrew in middle age and helped Jerome translate the Bible.",
  tryit=["Trace how the Bible came to you.", "Name the people between you and the original.", "Say one thing you will do to read it better."],
  saintStory="Paula was born in 347 into a noble Roman family. She married a senator and had five children. When her husband died in 379, she gave herself to prayer and charity. In 382, she hosted at her house a visiting group of bishops, including Jerome, a learned monk who was working for Pope Damasus.\nPaula and her daughter Eustochium became his students. They studied Scripture with him, even learning Hebrew, which was unusual for Roman women. After Damasus died, Jerome was driven from Rome, and in 385 he sailed for the Holy Land. Paula and Eustochium followed him, leaving behind her other children. In Bethlehem, they founded two monasteries, one for men and one for women, near the Church of the Nativity, with a guesthouse for pilgrims. Paula spent her fortune on the poor.\nShe worked alongside Jerome, who translated the Bible into Latin, the Vulgate. He dedicated many of his Bible commentaries to Paula and Eustochium, and said Paula pressed him to keep going. She died in 404. Jerome wrote her biography.",
  script=dict(
    open="Ask: “How do you think the Bible got into the book you hold? Who wrote it? Who decided what was in it? Who translated it?” Let people share what they know and what they have heard. Write the questions that come up on a board.",
    teach=["Say: “The Bible is not one book but a library of 73 books (46 Old Testament, 27 New) written over about 1,000 years, in Hebrew, Aramaic and Greek, by many human authors, with God as the principal author (2 Timothy 3:16; Dei Verbum 11). The Old Testament took shape over centuries, from oral tradition, court records, prophetic oracles, songs, laws. The New Testament was written in the first century, between about AD 50 and 100: Paul’s letters first (1 Thessalonians around AD 50), then the Gospels (Mark around 65-70; Matthew and Luke around 80-85; John around 90-100). The Gospels came from the preaching and memories of eyewitnesses, shaped for particular churches.”", "Say: “The canon, the list of inspired books, was recognized by the Church over time. By around AD 200 the four Gospels and most of Paul’s letters were widely used. Athanasius’s Easter letter in 367 lists the 27 books we have; the Councils of Hippo (393) and Carthage (397) approved it. Why those books? The Church looked for apostolic origin, orthodoxy of teaching, and use in the liturgy. The Old Testament canon: the Church used the Greek Septuagint, which included the books Protestants call apocrypha (the deuterocanonical books: Tobit, Judith, Wisdom, Sirach, Baruch, 1-2 Maccabees, parts of Daniel and Esther). Luther dropped them in the 16th century. Trent defined the canon in 1546. Other early writings, like the Gospel of Thomas, were rejected because they were later, not apostolic, and taught ideas contrary to the apostolic faith.”", "Say: “How reliable is the text? We have no original manuscripts, but we have an enormous number of copies: over 5,800 Greek New Testament manuscripts, some from the second century (the John Rylands fragment, c. 125), and many early translations. The vast majority of variations are minor, like spelling. Textual scholarship allows us to be highly confident of what the authors wrote. The Old Testament: the Dead Sea Scrolls (found 1947) confirmed the accuracy of the Hebrew text over a thousand years. Translations: Jerome’s Vulgate was the standard for a thousand years; English translations like the Douay-Rheims, RSV-CE, NABRE, and ESV-CE. Reading the Bible well: with the Church, in context, with attention to literary form, and in prayer. Paula’s story shows that women and men have always given their lives to bring the Scriptures to the people.”"],
    ask=["How many books are in the Catholic Bible? (73: 46 Old Testament and 27 New.)", "Who recognized the canon? (The Church, over centuries, at councils like Hippo, Carthage and Trent.)", "How reliable is the New Testament text? (Very: thousands of manuscripts, some from the second century.)"],
    practice=["Draw a timeline from Abraham to AD 400. Mark when each part of the Bible was written and when the canon was recognized.", "In pairs, trace how your own Bible reached you: translator, publisher, bookstore, giver. Name the chain.", "Compare a Catholic Bible and a Protestant Bible’s table of contents and mark the differences.", "Each person makes one commitment: a daily time and place to read a short passage this week."],
    close="Pray the prayer before reading Scripture: “Speak, Lord, your servant is listening.” Sign of the Cross. Ask each person to bring a Bible next time, and note which translation they use.")),

'YA-2-2': dict(
  saint="Hegesippus: a second-century traveler who checked the churches’ teaching against Rome’s.",
  tryit=["List three beliefs you hold and where you learned them.", "Trace each back as far as you can.", "Say who vouches for them."],
  saintStory="Hegesippus was a Christian of Jewish background who lived in the second century. He was concerned about growing confusion: various teachers were spreading secret doctrines and claiming to be the true Christianity. In about 155, he decided to find out for himself what the churches taught. He traveled from the East to Rome, visiting many cities and bishops along the way, including Corinth, where he stayed for some time.\nHe found, he reported, that in every church he visited, they taught the same thing: “In every succession and in every city, the teaching is in accordance with the Law and the Prophets and the Lord.” When he reached Rome, he made a list of the bishops in succession, from the apostles to the present. He wrote five books of Memoirs, now lost except for quotations preserved by the historian Eusebius.\nHis journey is a snapshot of how early Christians knew they had the true faith: not by private revelation, but by the continuity of teaching in the churches founded by the apostles.",
  script=dict(
    open="Write three beliefs on the board, such as the Trinity, the Eucharist, and Sunday Mass. Ask the group, “Where did you first hear each one? Who vouched for it?” Take a few examples.",
    teach=["Say: “Catholic teaching on revelation: God revealed himself fully in Jesus Christ. This revelation is handed on in two ways, Sacred Scripture and Sacred Tradition, which ‘flow from the same divine wellspring’ and make up a single deposit of faith (DV 9-10). The Church’s teaching authority, the Magisterium, interprets this deposit. It doesn’t add new revelation but guards, explains and applies it. The Catechism: ‘The task of interpreting the Word of God authentically has been entrusted solely to the Magisterium of the Church, that is, to the Pope and to the bishops in communion with him.’ (CCC 85) Why? Because Jesus promised to be with the Church until the end, and the Spirit would guide her ‘into all the truth.’ (John 16:13)”", "Say: “Tradition is not mere old customs. The Greek paradosis means ‘handing on.’ Paul: ‘I delivered to you as of first importance what I also received.’ (1 Corinthians 15:3) ‘Hold to the traditions which you were taught by us, either by word of mouth or by letter.’ (2 Thessalonians 2:15) The apostles taught by preaching, by example, by liturgy; some of this was written down in the New Testament, and some was handed on in the Church’s life: creeds, worship, the canon of Scripture itself. Tradition is the living memory of the Church, guided by the Spirit. The test: continuity with the apostles (Vincent of Lérins: what has been believed everywhere, always, by all). Tradition allows development, like an acorn to an oak, but never contradiction.”", "Say: “Why not Scripture alone? Because the Bible doesn’t say that it alone is the authority; because someone must say which books belong; because Scripture needs interpretation, and sincere Christians disagree; and because the early Church lived by the apostolic teaching before the New Testament was finished. Protestant Christians honor Scripture deeply; the difference is about how its meaning is guarded. The Magisterium has levels: dogmas (infallible); definitive doctrines; authoritative non-definitive teaching requiring religious assent; prudential judgments. The laity are not passive: the whole Church has a sense of the faith (sensus fidei). It is reasonable to wonder, ‘Why should I trust this?’ The answer: Christ’s promise, and the evidence of two thousand years of continuity. For you: ask, study, and also trust.”"],
    ask=["What are the two ways revelation is handed on? (Scripture and Tradition.)", "What is the Magisterium? (The Pope and bishops in communion with him, who interpret the deposit of faith.)", "What test did Hegesippus use? (Continuity of teaching with the apostles in the churches.)"],
    practice=["Each person lists three beliefs they hold and writes where they learned them (parent, teacher, Scripture, experience).", "Take one belief and trace it back: who taught the one who taught you? How far can you get? Where does the Church vouch for it?", "In pairs, explain the difference between Tradition and traditions.", "Write a two-sentence answer to the question: “Why trust the Church’s teaching?”"],
    close="Pray the Nicene Creed together slowly. Sign of the Cross. Ask the group to read CCC 74-100 this week and underline one line that surprised them.")),

'YA-2-3': dict(
  saint="Venerable Mary Ward: a woman who loved the Church so much that she kept obeying while it jailed her.",
  tryit=["List what bothers you about the Church.", "List what you would lose without it.", "Say what you owe and what you would change."],
  saintStory="Mary Ward was born in 1585 in Yorkshire, England, in a Catholic family during the time when Catholicism was illegal. She grew up hearing of martyrs and hiding priests. At fifteen, she felt a call to the religious life, and crossed to Flanders, where she became a lay sister among the Poor Clares. Soon she felt God wanted something else: a community of women, living without enclosure, who would teach girls and serve the Church actively in the world, as the Jesuits did.\nIn 1609 she began her Institute of the Blessed Virgin Mary, with schools for girls in Saint-Omer and elsewhere. The idea of women religious teaching and traveling freely was unacceptable to many bishops. Critics attacked her, and in 1631 Pope Urban VIII suppressed her institute. She was arrested by the Inquisition and imprisoned in a convent in Munich, as a “heretic, schismatic and rebel to Holy Church.”\nShe wrote, from her sickbed, to the pope, protesting her loyalty. She was released, and spent the rest of her life quietly in England, dying in Yorkshire in 1645. Her work survived, and the Institute was approved in 1877. She was declared Venerable in 2009. Her legacy shows what it means to love the Church even when she is wrong.",
  script=dict(
    open="Say: “Be honest. Name something about the Church that frustrates or hurts you.” Go around, and write each answer on a sheet. Receive them without defending. After everyone has spoken, say, “Now name one thing that you would be poorer without.”",
    teach=["Say: “‘I like Jesus but not the Church’ is a very common view, and it deserves a serious answer. First, the Church is not an optional add-on. Jesus founded a community: he called twelve, gave Peter the keys, sent them to baptize and teach, and said he would be with them. Paul says the Church is the Body of Christ: ‘Saul, Saul, why do you persecute me?’ (Acts 9:4) Jesus identifies himself with his people. You can’t be a Christian alone: Baptism incorporates you into a body. St. Cyprian: ‘He cannot have God for his Father who has not the Church for his mother.’”", "Say: “What does the Church give that we can’t give ourselves? The Word of God (the Bible comes from her); the sacraments (Baptism, Eucharist, Confession); the truth, guarded and handed on; community and mission; the saints; correction when we go astray. Without a community, faith tends to become private opinion. Without sacraments, we can’t receive the bread of life. We don’t invent Christianity; we receive it. And the Church is for the world, running hospitals, schools, charities. The Church feeds the poor in more places than any other organization.”", "Say: “But the Church is also full of sinners, and her history includes serious failures: corruption, abuse, wars, cover-ups, injustice. The Church doesn’t deny it. The Catechism (CCC 827): ‘The Church, embracing sinners in her own bosom, is at once holy and always in need of purification.’ Her holiness comes from Christ. Jesus knew his Church would include weeds and wheat (Matthew 13:24-30), a net with good and bad fish (13:47-50). He chose Judas. Catherine of Siena and Mary Ward show how to respond: neither leave nor pretend. Stay, speak the truth with love, work for reform from within, and keep your own life holy. Pope Francis described the Church as a ‘field hospital’: not for the perfect but the wounded. A key question: Is the problem the Church or specific people in the Church? Both can need healing. And if you have been hurt, the Church should hear you.”"],
    ask=["Did Jesus found a Church? (Yes: the Twelve, the keys to Peter, the commission to baptize and teach.)", "What does the Church give that we can’t give ourselves? (The Bible, sacraments, guarded teaching, community, saints.)", "How can the Church be holy and sinful? (Her holiness is Christ’s; her members are sinners.)"],
    practice=["In pairs, each person shares their honest frustration with the Church and what they would lose without her.", "On a sheet, list five gifts the Church has given you, even indirectly.", "Write one thing you would change and one thing you will do toward it, such as praying, serving or speaking to your pastor.", "Share one commitment with the group."],
    close="Pray for the Church: “Lord, purify your Church and make her holy, beginning with me.” Sign of the Cross. Encourage the group to read about one saint who reformed the Church.")),
}
